{"seconds": 47.1, "exit": 0, "final": "After Ke2, same story\u2014Black plays Qxa3 and wins a pawn. So **Qa1+** forces Kf2 (or Ke2), then Qxa3, and Black wins cleanly.\n\nThe motif is a **deflection/attraction** of the king, followed by capturing the loose pawn. The key tactic is that the queen forks the king and the pawn, forcing the king to move, allowing Black to grab material.\n\n**Best move:** Qa1+\n\n**Main line:** Qa1+ Kf2 Qxa3\n\n**Motif:** deflection\n\n**Coaching Note (80 words):**\nAfter White plays Qc2, scan for checks that attack loose pieces. Here, Qa1+ is a forcing check that also attacks the undefended a3-pawn. White's king must move (Kf2 or Ke2), and then Black captures with Qxa3, winning a clean pawn. The key lesson: when your opponent's pieces lack defenders, a check that *also* attacks something loose is often the strongest move. Look for these \"double-duty\" checks.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Qa1+\", \"line\": [\"Qa1+\", \"Kf2\", \"Qxa3\"], \"motifs\": [\"deflection\", \"hangingPiece\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p1q2/1p4p1/5b2/PP5R/2Q5/4K3 b - - 3 54"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p1q2/1p4p1/5b2/PP5R/2Q5/4K3 b - - 3 54"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p1q2/1p4p1/5b2/PP5R/2Q5/4K3 b - - 3 54"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p1q2/1p4p1/5b2/PP5R/2Q5/4K3 b - - 3 54", "move": "Qc3+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p1q2/1p4p1/5b2/PP5R/2Q5/4K3 b - - 3 54", "move": "Bg3+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p1q2/1p4p1/5b2/PP5R/2Q5/4K3 b - - 3 54", "move": "Qe5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p4/1p4p1/5b2/PPq4R/2Q5/4K3 w - - 4 55", "move": "Qxc3"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/6k1/p2p4/1p4p1/5b2/PPq4R/2Q5/4K3 w - - 4 55", "move": "Rxc3"}}, {"tool